\begin{align*} \label{eq:1} \text{Let } a \text{ and } b \text{ be positive integers, neither of which is a multiple of } 3. \text{ Find the least possible remainder when } a^2 + b^2 \text{ is divided by } 3. \end{align*} 
We know that the remainder when an integer is divided by 3 can be 0, 1, or 2. 
We also know that the square of an integer has the same remainder when divided by 3 as the integer itself. 
So, $a^2 \equiv r_a \pmod{3}$ and $b^2 \equiv r_b \pmod{3}$, where $r_a, r_b \in \{0, 1, 2\}$.
Since $a$ and $b$ are not multiples of 3, we have $a \not\equiv 0 \pmod{3}$ and $b \not\equiv 0 \pmod{3}$. 
Therefore, $a^2 \equiv 1 \pmod{3}$ and $b^2 \equiv 1 \pmod{3}$.
So, $a^2 + b^2 \equiv 1 + 1 \pmod{3}$, which means $a^2 + b^2 \equiv 2 \pmod{3}$.
Thus, the least possible remainder when $a^2 + b^2$ is divided by 3 is 2.

\end{align*} 
